# Silent channel 15 Nov 2026 – free-text recalls. (post, q, options, answer, key, explanation, note)
R = [
(88429,"Old woman presented with lower GI bleeding. On examination: diffusely tender abdomen and perianal exam showing fresh blood. The patient is stable. What is the most appropriate management?",["Laparotomy","Angiography","Urgent colonoscopy","Blood transfusion"],"C","Stable LGIB → colonoscopy","Stable = colonoscopy (diagnostic + therapeutic). Unstable / massive bleed = CT angiography/angiography. Laparotomy only if bleeding source can't be controlled.","التعليقات: Stable = C, Unstable = A/B"),
(88435,"A patient develops itching (urticaria) during a blood transfusion. Vitals are stable. What is the most appropriate management?",["Stop the transfusion permanently and send blood for culture","Antihistamine, then resume the transfusion slowly (premedicate next time)","Warm the blood","IM epinephrine and IV hydrocortisone"],"B","Mild allergic (urticarial) reaction → antihistamine","Isolated itching/urticaria with normal vitals = mild allergic reaction: pause, give antihistamine, resume slowly; premedicate with antihistamine for future transfusions. Epinephrine is for anaphylaxis (hypotension, wheeze).","الخيارات الأصلية: Warm blood / Premedication antihistamines"),
(88437,"A 65-year-old woman presents with abdominal pain and distension. Abdominal X-ray shows a dilated segment of the large colon measuring 9 cm with gas outlining the haustral markings. What is the most appropriate next diagnostic investigation?",["MRI abdomen","Barium enema","Exploratory laparotomy","CT abdomen with contrast"],"D","Large bowel dilatation → CT","CT abdomen with contrast confirms the cause (volvulus vs obstruction vs pseudo-obstruction) and looks for ischemia/perforation. Barium enema is avoided when perforation is possible.","التعليقات: D"),
(88438,"65-year-old patient, post-subtotal colectomy day 3: Temp 38.5 °C, HR 96, RR 18, BP 125/78, WBC 15,000. Alert, hemodynamically stable, no organ dysfunction. What is the most appropriate classification?",["Sepsis","Systemic inflammatory response syndrome (SIRS)","Septic shock","Severe sepsis"],"B","≥2 SIRS criteria + no organ dysfunction = SIRS","SIRS: temp >38, HR >90, RR >20, WBC >12k (≥2 criteria). Sepsis needs infection + organ dysfunction (Sepsis-3). Here no confirmed infection and no organ dysfunction → SIRS.",""),
(88440,"Female in her twenties presents with shortness of breath and chest pain. Medically free, on OCPs. Stable. What is the next step in management?",["Anticoagulant drugs","Thrombolytic drugs"],"A","Stable PE → anticoagulation","OCP = risk factor for PE. Hemodynamically stable PE → anticoagulation. Thrombolysis is reserved for massive PE (hypotension/shock).","التعليقات: A"),
(88441,"Pedestrian trauma victim brought by the Red Crescent. Decreased air entry. BP 60/44, SpO2 76%, RR 37. What is the next step in management?",["Thoracotomy","Needle thoracostomy","Chest tube insertion"],"B","Tension pneumothorax → needle decompression first","Decreased air entry + shock + hypoxia in trauma = tension pneumothorax → immediate needle (or finger) decompression, then chest tube.","التعليقات: B"),
(88442,"Child with head trauma vomited, then came to ED drowsy with a dilated pupil. CT shows a small epidural hematoma with no midline shift. What is the most appropriate management?",["Urgent surgical evacuation","Repeat CT","Mannitol"],"A","Signs of herniation → evacuation","Drowsiness + dilated pupil = clinical herniation → urgent evacuation regardless of hematoma size on CT.",""),
(88444,"An 8-week incomplete abortion with ongoing bleeding. What is the most appropriate management?",["Conservative management","Surgical evacuation (D&C / MVA)"],"A","Stable incomplete abortion → expectant/conservative is acceptable","Evacuation only if heavy bleeding, instability or infection.","✔ الحمراني 15 نوفمبر: Conservative"),
(88444,"Down syndrome fetus – which pregnancy finding is associated (due to duodenal atresia)?",["Oligohydramnios","Polyhydramnios","Macrosomia","Post-term pregnancy"],"B","Down → duodenal atresia → polyhydramnios","Fetus can't swallow/absorb amniotic fluid because of GI obstruction (duodenal atresia) → polyhydramnios.",""),
(88444,"Premature ovarian insufficiency – what is the major long-term risk?",["Osteoporosis","Endometrial cancer","Breast cancer","Ovarian cancer"],"A","POI = low estrogen → osteoporosis","Estrogen deficiency → bone loss (and CVD). Give HRT until the age of natural menopause.",""),
(88444,"Term neonate with meconium aspiration syndrome on maximal ventilation develops persistent pulmonary hypertension. Best next treatment?",["Inhaled nitric oxide","Surfactant only","Indomethacin","Steroids"],"A","MAS + PPHN → inhaled NO","Inhaled nitric oxide is a selective pulmonary vasodilator for PPHN; ECMO if it fails.",""),
(88444,"Monochorionic diamniotic twins – when did the embryo divide?",["0–72 hours (days 0–3)","Days 4–8","Days 8–12","After day 13"],"B","Mono-Di = days 4–8","Di-Di 0–3 days, Mono-Di 4–8 days, Mono-Mono 8–12 days, conjoined >13 days.",""),
(88444,"Most common respiratory problem in a term baby born by elective C-section?",["Respiratory distress syndrome","Transient tachypnea of the newborn","Meconium aspiration","Pneumothorax"],"B","C-section → TTN","No labor → delayed clearance of lung fluid → TTN (resolves in 24–72 h).",""),
(88444,"Preterm baby with symptomatic PDA – medical treatment?",["Indomethacin (or ibuprofen)","Prostaglandin E1","Digoxin","Propranolol"],"A","Close PDA → indomethacin","NSAIDs (indomethacin/ibuprofen) or paracetamol close the PDA. PGE1 keeps it OPEN (duct-dependent lesions).",""),
(88444,"Agent used for cervical ripening at term?",["Oxytocin","Prostaglandin E2 (dinoprostone)","Magnesium sulfate","Methylergometrine"],"B","Cervical ripening → PGE2","PGE2 (dinoprostone) or misoprostol (PGE1) ripen the cervix; oxytocin is for induction/augmentation once favourable.",""),
(88444,"After manual removal of the placenta, what should be given?",["Prophylactic antibiotics","Methotrexate","Anti-D for all","Nothing"],"A","Manual removal → prophylactic antibiotics","Intrauterine manipulation → single dose prophylactic antibiotic to prevent endometritis.",""),
(88444,"Baby born at 29 weeks with respiratory distress. Initial respiratory support?",["CPAP (± surfactant)","Immediate intubation for all","Oxygen by nasal cannula only","Observe"],"A","Preterm RDS → CPAP early, surfactant if needed","Start CPAP early; give surfactant if FiO2 requirement rises (>30%) or intubated.","التجميع: surfactant or CPAP"),
(88445,"18-month boy with one week of shortness of breath; viral infection a week ago. Hepatomegaly, gallop rhythm, hyperactive precordium, abnormal CXR. Most appropriate next step?",["ECG","Echocardiography","Chest X-ray","Virology screening"],"B","Post-viral heart failure → myocarditis → Echo","Viral illness then heart failure (gallop, hepatomegaly, cardiomegaly) = viral myocarditis/DCM. Echo confirms ventricular dysfunction.","التعليقات: Echo"),
(88447,"A 62-year-old woman on apixaban for DVT fell and developed an intracranial hemorrhage. INR 1.2. Most appropriate management?",["Vitamin K","PCC with factor X (4-factor PCC / andexanet)","Activated factor VII","FFP and vitamin K"],"B","DOAC (Xa inhibitor) bleeding → andexanet or 4F-PCC","Apixaban = factor Xa inhibitor; INR is unreliable. Reverse with andexanet alfa or 4-factor PCC. Vitamin K/FFP are for warfarin.",""),
(88450,"Female with headache and high BP started on hydrochlorothiazide, nifedipine and candesartan. 2 weeks later she presents with low urine output. Initial management?",["Stop candesartan","Switch nifedipine to amlodipine","Start hemodialysis"],"A","ARB → AKI (renal artery stenosis) → stop ARB","AKI after starting ACEi/ARB suggests bilateral renal artery stenosis → stop the ARB.","التعليقات: A"),
(88450,"Old man with osteoarthritis, DM and HTN presents with chest pain. Troponin, ECG and labs normal. Next step to establish the diagnosis?",["Dobutamine stress echo","Exercise stress ECG","Coronary angiography","PCI"],"A","Can't exercise (OA) → pharmacological stress","Osteoarthritis = can't run on a treadmill → dobutamine stress echo (or pharmacological nuclear scan).","التعليقات: A – because of osteoarthritis"),
(88450,"Woman with depression and HTN presents with irritability, insomnia and fatigue for 2 weeks. On fluoxetine and hydrochlorothiazide. Next step?",["Check TSH and thyroid function","Change antidepressant","Refer to psychiatry"],"A","Rule out organic cause (thyroid) first","New mood/sleep/energy symptoms → exclude hyperthyroidism (and electrolytes) before changing psychiatric treatment.","التعليقات: A"),
(88450,"Diabetic man recently started on amitriptyline for neuropathic pain develops difficulty initiating urination and incomplete emptying. Mechanism?",["Anticholinergic effect of amitriptyline","Diabetic autonomic neuropathy","Serotonin syndrome","Alpha-agonist effect"],"A","TCA → anticholinergic → urinary retention","TCAs block muscarinic receptors → urinary retention, dry mouth, constipation, blurred vision.",""),
(88450,"Woman with rheumatic heart disease for dental extraction. Which prophylactic antibiotic?",["Ciprofloxacin","Clindamycin","Ampicillin","Doxycycline"],"C","Endocarditis prophylaxis → amoxicillin/ampicillin","First choice amoxicillin; if not in options, ampicillin (IV/IM). Clindamycin only if penicillin allergy.","التعليقات: Ampicillin"),
(88450,"80-year-old female with frequency, urgency and dysuria. Examination shows grade 2 pelvic organ prolapse. Next step?",["Hysterectomy","Mid-urethral sling","Urinalysis","Anterior and posterior colporrhaphy"],"C","Urinary symptoms → rule out UTI first","Dysuria + frequency → urinalysis/culture before attributing symptoms to prolapse.","التعليقات: C"),
(88450,"6-year-old with lethargy, weakness and headache. 1 week ago bloody diarrhea, abdominal pain and fever. BP 130/80, Hb 90, urinalysis RBC and protein. Which culture will most likely confirm the diagnosis?",["CSF","Stool","Urine","Blood"],"B","HUS after bloody diarrhea → stool culture (E. coli O157:H7)","Hemolytic anemia + AKI (hematuria, proteinuria, HTN) after bloody diarrhea = HUS from Shiga-toxin E. coli → stool culture.","التعليقات: B"),
(88450,"32-year-old G4P2, pregnant, diagnosed with primary HSV and treated with 10 days of acyclovir. Rationale?",["Decrease viral shedding and duration of infection","Eradicate herpes simplex virus","Decrease transplacental transmission"],"A","Acyclovir → less shedding & shorter attack","Antivirals don't eradicate latent virus; neonatal herpes is mainly from the birth canal, not transplacental.","التعليقات: A"),
(88450,"60-year-old man with jaundice for 2 months. US: liver cirrhosis with 2 lesions. Cause of his condition?",["Aflatoxin","Lead poisoning","Wilson disease","Chronic hepatitis B"],"D","Cirrhosis + liver lesions (HCC) → chronic HBV","Most common cause of cirrhosis + HCC in Saudi Arabia = chronic hepatitis B.","التعليقات: D. ⚠ عند الحمراني نفس السؤال بدون خيار HBV وجوابه Aflatoxin، فإذا ما جاء HBV اختاري Aflatoxin"),
(88451,"Post-subtotal colectomy patient with NG tube on high continuous suction. K 2.8. Most likely cause of hypokalemia?",["Nasogastric suctioning","Inadequate dietary intake","Renal potassium wasting due to aldosterone","Intraoperative fluid shift"],"C","NG loss → alkalosis + hypovolemia → aldosterone → renal K loss","Gastric fluid has little K. Loss of HCl → metabolic alkalosis + volume depletion → aldosterone → kidneys waste K. That is the main mechanism.","⚠ التعليقات: C. الجواب السهل A (NG suction)، والدقيق C"),
(88452,"Saudi man, 34, with headache and back pain; Brucella serology positive (spondylitis). How long should treatment continue?",["3 weeks","6 weeks","12 weeks","15 weeks"],"B","Uncomplicated brucellosis → 6 weeks","Doxycycline + rifampicin/gentamicin for 6 weeks. Headache and back pain alone are NOT signs of complicated brucellosis; spondylitis/neurobrucellosis → ≥12 weeks.","✔ الحمراني 15 نوفمبر: 6 weeks"),
(88452,"Child with fever and sandpaper skin rash. Causative organism?",["Streptococcus pyogenes","Streptococcus pneumoniae","Haemophilus influenzae","Staphylococcus aureus"],"A","Sandpaper rash = scarlet fever = GAS","Scarlet fever: group A strep (S. pyogenes) toxin. Strawberry tongue, Pastia lines. Treat with penicillin/amoxicillin.","التعليقات: S. pyogenes"),
(88453,"Long scenario: anemia with leg ulcer, splenomegaly, gallstones (calculous cholecystitis), blood smear image. Diagnosis?",["Sickle cell anemia","Beta thalassemia","Hereditary spherocytosis","B12 deficiency"],"C","Hemolysis + gallstones + splenomegaly → HS","Chronic hemolysis → pigment gallstones; splenomegaly persists in HS (adult SCA usually has autosplenectomy). Leg ulcers can occur in HS. Decide by the smear: spherocytes = HS, sickle cells = SCA.","⚠ ما فيه جواب مؤكد، يعتمد على الصورة"),
(88454,"Child with paroxysmal cough, sometimes followed by vomiting. Management?",["Azithromycin","Ceftriaxone","Steroids","Sedation"],"A","Pertussis → azithromycin","Paroxysms + post-tussive vomiting = pertussis → macrolide (azithromycin) for patient and contacts.",""),
(88454,"Pregnant woman with leukocytes on urinalysis/positive urine culture but no symptoms. Management?",["No treatment","Antibiotics even without symptoms","Repeat culture after delivery"],"B","Asymptomatic bacteriuria in pregnancy → treat","Untreated bacteriuria → pyelonephritis and preterm labor; always treat in pregnancy.",""),
(88454,"Child with nasal discharge, sore throat, pharyngeal erythema, tonsillar enlargement without exudate. Rapid strep test positive. Management?",["Amoxicillin/penicillin for 10 days","Symptomatic only","Azithromycin for 3 days","Tonsillectomy"],"A","Positive strep → penicillin/amoxicillin 10 days","Treat a positive test regardless of exudate to prevent rheumatic fever.",""),
(88454,"15-year-old female with primary amenorrhea; examination shows a bluish bulging membrane at the introitus. Management?",["Incision of the hymen (hymenotomy)","OCP","Karyotype","Reassure"],"A","Imperforate hymen → hymenotomy","Cyclic pain + blue bulge = hematocolpos from imperforate hymen → cruciate incision.",""),
(88454,"Girl with primary amenorrhea, normal breasts but absent axillary/pubic hair. Diagnosis?",["Androgen insensitivity syndrome","Turner syndrome","Mullerian agenesis","Kallmann syndrome"],"A","Breasts + no hair = AIS","46,XY with androgen receptor defect: breasts (aromatized testosterone) but no pubic/axillary hair, blind vagina. Mullerian agenesis has normal hair.",""),
(88454,"Most common cause of death in hemodialysis patients?",["Cardiovascular disease","Infection","Hyperkalemia","Bleeding"],"A","HD death → cardiovascular","Cardiovascular disease is the leading cause; infection is second.",""),
(88454,"Child had a simple febrile seizure. The mother is worried. Most appropriate action?",["Reassure the mother and teach her how to deal with future seizures","Start prophylactic phenobarbital","EEG and MRI","Start valproate"],"A","Simple febrile seizure → reassure + educate","Benign; no anticonvulsants. Teach first aid; also look for the source of fever.",""),
(88454,"Trauma patient with paradoxical chest wall movement. Diagnosis?",["Flail chest","Tension pneumothorax","Hemothorax","Cardiac tamponade"],"A","Paradoxical movement = flail chest","≥2 fractures in ≥2 adjacent ribs → segment moves in with inspiration. Treat with analgesia + O2; ventilate only if failing.",""),
(88454,"Boy with sudden scrotal pain while playing. Diagnosis?",["Testicular torsion","Epididymitis","Torsion of appendix testis","Hydrocele"],"A","Sudden pain during activity → torsion","Surgical emergency: explore within 6 hours, don't wait for Doppler if classic.",""),
(88454,"Infant with a palpable mass in the inguinal area and empty right scrotum (undescended testis). Management?",["Wait until 5 years","Observation","Surgical intervention (orchidopexy)"],"C","Undescended testis → orchidopexy by 6–18 months","Spontaneous descent rarely occurs after 6 months; orchidopexy by 6–18 months reduces infertility/malignancy risk.",""),
(88454,"Committee for cancer screening: which cancer is most important to screen for?",["Colon cancer","Lung cancer","Pancreatic cancer"],"A","Screening program → colorectal","Colorectal cancer is common, has a long pre-malignant phase and effective screening. No screening for pancreatic cancer.",""),
(88455,"At what age is a child routinely screened for iron-deficiency anemia?",["12 months","6 months","2 years","At birth"],"A","Anemia screening at 12 months","Universal Hb screening at around 12 months (AAP).",""),
(88455,"Anal fissure with pain on defecation. Initial management?",["Topical diltiazem","Lateral internal sphincterotomy","Botox","Hemorrhoidectomy"],"A","Fissure → conservative + topical CCB/nitrate","Fiber, sitz baths, and topical diltiazem/GTN first. Surgery only if chronic and failed.",""),
(88455,"Patient with symptomatic hemorrhoids causing anemia. Management?",["Hemorrhoidectomy","Rubber band ligation","Fiber only","Sclerotherapy"],"A","Hemorrhoids with anemia → hemorrhoidectomy","Complicated (anemia) / large grade III–IV hemorrhoids → surgical hemorrhoidectomy.",""),
(88455,"Hours after thyroidectomy, a patient develops neck swelling at the incision site with respiratory distress. Management?",["Bedside wound opening and evacuation of hematoma","CT neck","Intubate and observe","IV antibiotics"],"A","Post-thyroidectomy hematoma → open at bedside","Expanding hematoma compresses the airway → open the sutures immediately at the bedside, then go to OR.",""),
(88455,"A nurse was about to give a DKA patient insulin, but on reading the order before giving it she found it was written IM instead of IV. What is this event?",["Near miss","Adverse event","Sentinel event","No-harm event"],"A","Error caught before reaching the patient = near miss","Near miss: error intercepted before reaching the patient. No-harm event: reached the patient without harm.",""),
(88455,"Pregnant woman with fetal distress; doctor advises urgent C-section, but she refuses and wants a second opinion. What to do?",["Respect her decision and arrange a second opinion","Sedate her and do the surgery","Take the husband's consent and do the surgery","Pressure her to accept"],"A","Competent adult refusal → respect it","A competent woman's autonomy covers decisions about her own body, even if the fetus is at risk. Explain risks and document, and facilitate the second opinion.",""),
(88456,"Pregnant woman develops headache and blurred vision. BP 160/80. Best next drug?",["Methyldopa","Hydralazine","Magnesium sulfate","Amlodipine"],"B","Systolic ≥160 = severe-range BP → IV hydralazine/labetalol now","BP ≥160 must be lowered within 30–60 min (hydralazine/labetalol/nifedipine); MgSO4 is also given for seizure prophylaxis, but the 'best drug' for the BP is hydralazine.","✔ جواب الحمراني (نفس السؤال 160/110 → Hydralazine). التعليقات: MgSO4 أكثر، وواحد hydralazine"),
(88456,"Woman with irregular cycles and 9 weeks of amenorrhea presents with sudden sharp left iliac fossa pain radiating to the shoulder, abdominal tenderness and rigidity, hypotensive. How would you manage?",["Observation","Laparoscopy","Laparotomy","Methotrexate"],"C","Ruptured ectopic + unstable → laparotomy","Shoulder-tip pain + rigidity = hemoperitoneum. Unstable → laparotomy; stable → laparoscopy.","⚠ لو مستقرة = laparoscopy"),
(88456,"Molar pregnancy treated; on methotrexate, β-hCG 1900 → 3000 → 1800. Next step?",["Observation (serial hCG)","Laparotomy","Suction evacuation","Another dose of methotrexate"],"C","Molar pregnancy is treated by suction evacuation","A mole on MTX without evacuation makes no sense – evacuate (assuming not done yet), then follow hCG.","⚠ تحديث الحمراني (16 نوف): A vs C، ويميل لـ Suction evacuation"),
(88456,"After suction evacuation of a molar pregnancy, β-hCG plateaus/rises on follow-up. Next step?",["Refer to oncology for methotrexate (GTN)","Repeat evacuation","Hysterectomy","Observe"],"A","Plateau/rise after mole → GTN → chemotherapy","Persistent GTN after evacuation → staging and methotrexate.",""),
(88456,"ECG shows SVT with an unstable patient. Management?",["Synchronized cardioversion","Adenosine","Verapamil","Amiodarone"],"A","Unstable SVT → cardioversion","Stable: vagal maneuvers → adenosine. Unstable: synchronized cardioversion.","التجميع: cardioversion"),
(88456,"Child eating popcorn, then sudden continuous cough, localized wheeze and decreased air entry on one side. Diagnosis?",["Foreign body aspiration","Asthma","Pneumonia","Croup"],"A","Sudden cough + unilateral wheeze → FB","Next: rigid bronchoscopy.",""),
(88456,"Unconscious person found in a burning house; burns on the face, soot at the nostrils and mouth. Diagnosis/priority?",["Inhalation injury (and CO poisoning) → early intubation","Look for carbon dioxide poisoning","Only facial burns"],"A","Soot + facial burns → inhalation injury","Secure the airway early; give 100% O2 for CO poisoning.",""),
(88456,"Chest trauma; right side dull to percussion. Diagnosis and management?",["Hemothorax → chest tube","Pneumothorax → needle","Flail chest → ventilation","Contusion → observe"],"A","Dull percussion after trauma = hemothorax","Chest tube; thoracotomy if >1500 mL initially or >200 mL/h.",""),
(88456,"Patient with fever for one month; CSF: low glucose, high protein, mainly lymphocytes. Diagnosis?",["Tuberculous meningitis","Bacterial meningitis","Viral meningitis","Fungal meningitis"],"A","Chronic + lymphocytes + low glucose → TB","Viral: normal glucose. Bacterial: neutrophils, acute.","⚠ من الذاكرة"),
(88456,"Diabetic patient develops hypertension. Best treatment?",["ACE inhibitor","Beta blocker","Thiazide","Calcium channel blocker"],"A","DM + HTN → ACEi/ARB","Renal protection (albuminuria).",""),
(88456,"SLE patient develops neuropsychiatric lupus. Treatment?",["High-dose steroids with cyclophosphamide","NSAIDs","Hydroxychloroquine alone","Observation"],"A","Severe organ involvement (CNS) → steroids + cyclophosphamide","Major organ (CNS/renal) lupus → pulse steroids + cyclophosphamide (or MMF for nephritis).",""),
(88456,"RA patient on prednisolone, methotrexate and HCQ still has active disease. What to add?",["Biologic (e.g., TNF inhibitor)","Azathioprine","Increase steroid long-term","Aspirin"],"A","Failed triple csDMARD → biologic","Persistent activity despite MTX + HCQ (+ steroid) → add a biologic (TNF inhibitor).","⚠ باقي الخيارات منسية"),
(88491,"40-year-old, 4 months after gastric bypass, presents with diffuse abdominal pain for 12 hours; tenderness and rebound tenderness. Next step?",["Gastrografin study","Emergency laparotomy","CT abdomen","Observation"],"B","Post-bypass + peritonitis → surgery","Peritonitis (rebound) after bariatric surgery = leak/internal hernia → urgent surgical exploration.",""),
(88457,"Woman with ectopic pregnancy 2 years ago, planning to conceive. Advice?",["Wait one year before conceiving","Take folic acid","IVF (ICSI)","Early localization of pregnancy (early US)"],"D","Previous ectopic → early US in next pregnancy","Recurrence risk ~10–15% → early localization of pregnancy by ultrasound.",""),
(88507,"18-year-old girl with irregular cycles and severe dysmenorrhea starting one day before menses for 3 days, missing work. Management?",["Paracetamol","OCP"],"B","Dysmenorrhea + irregular cycles → OCP","OCP treats both pain and cycle irregularity (NSAIDs are first-line for pain alone).",""),
(88517,"Patient with classic acute cholangitis (fever, jaundice, RUQ pain), BP normal. Most appropriate INITIAL management?",["ERCP","MRCP","IV crystalloid fluid bolus (+ IV antibiotics)"],"C","Cholangitis → resuscitate + antibiotics first","Initial: IV fluids + antibiotics. Then ERCP for decompression (definitive).","التعليقات: C"),
(88546,"Woman with ectopic treated with methotrexate: β-hCG day 1 = 1000, day 4 = 1200, day 7 = 2100. Next step?",["Surgical management","Repeat methotrexate","Observe","Repeat hCG in 1 week"],"A","hCG rising after MTX → surgery","Expect ≥15% fall between day 4 and 7; rising hCG = failure → surgery (or second dose if stable).","التجميع: surgical"),
(88546,"Surgeon doing a lipoma excision with lidocaine; patient develops tinnitus and perioral numbness. Diagnosis?",["Lidocaine toxicity","Anaphylaxis","Vasovagal","Hypoglycemia"],"A","Tinnitus + perioral numbness = LAST","Stop injection, support, lipid emulsion if severe.",""),
(88546,"Woman after severe postpartum hemorrhage cannot breastfeed and has amenorrhea. Diagnosis?",["Sheehan syndrome","Asherman syndrome","Prolactinoma","PCOS"],"A","PPH → no lactation → Sheehan","Pituitary infarction after PPH.",""),
(88547,"A study links BMI and systolic BP: SBP = 90 + 1.5 × BMI. Predicted SBP for BMI 30?",["120","135","150","170"],"B","90 + 1.5×30 = 135","Simple linear regression substitution.","التعليقات: 135"),
(88548,"9-month-old not sitting alone, can roll, no head lag when pulled up. What to do?",["Reassure","Reassess at next visit","Send for further work-up"],"C","Not sitting by 9 months = red flag","Sitting without support is expected by ~6–8 months; not by 9 months → refer/developmental work-up.","التعليقات: further work-up"),
(88549,"Picture of endometrial polyp. Management?",["Hysteroscopic polypectomy","D&C blind","Hysterectomy","OCP"],"A","Endometrial polyp → hysteroscopy + polypectomy","",""),
(88550,"Most common cause of postpartum hemorrhage?",["Uterine atony","Retained placenta","Genital tract trauma","Coagulopathy"],"A","PPH = Tone > Trauma > Tissue > Thrombin","",""),
(88551,"Stepping reflex – when does it disappear?",["By 2 months","By 4 months","By 6 months","By 12 months"],"A","Stepping disappears ~2 months","Rooting ~4 months, Moro ~4–6 months, palmar grasp ~6 months.",""),
(88552,"Infant with fatigue and hypotonia (hypoglycemia). Insulin 40 (high), cortisol 552 (normal), β-hydroxybutyrate 0.01 (low). Diagnosis?",["Glycogen storage disease","Congenital hyperinsulinism","Adrenal insufficiency","Growth hormone deficiency"],"B","Hypoglycemia + high insulin + low ketones → hyperinsulinism","Insulin suppresses ketogenesis. GSD and cortisol/GH deficiency have high ketones.",""),
(88553,"Type 1 diabetic in clinic is drowsy and sweating but able to swallow. Best immediate action?",["Give juice (oral fast-acting glucose)","IV dextrose","IM glucagon","Check HbA1c"],"A","Conscious hypoglycemia → oral glucose","Unable to swallow → IV dextrose or IM glucagon.",""),
(88554,"Murmur that increases with standing and decreases with handgrip. Diagnosis?",["Mitral valve prolapse","Rheumatic mitral regurgitation","Functional mitral regurgitation","Aortic stenosis"],"A","Louder with standing (↓preload) → MVP / HCM","MVP and HCM get louder with less preload (standing/Valsalva). Most murmurs (MR, AS) get softer with standing.",""),
(88555,"History of drinking unpasteurized milk with fever. Diagnosis?",["Brucellosis","Typhoid","Tuberculosis","Q fever"],"A","Raw milk + fever + back pain → Brucella","",""),
(88556,"Bloody nipple discharge from a single duct. Most likely cause?",["Intraductal papilloma","Duct ectasia","Breast cancer","Galactorrhea"],"A","Bloody single-duct discharge → intraductal papilloma","Work up with imaging (US/mammogram) ± ductography.",""),
(88557,"Man with SOB and fatigue associated with work days. Next step?",["Repeat spirometry after work exposure","Start inhaled steroids","Chest CT","Reassure"],"A","Occupational asthma → serial spirometry/PEF at and away from work","",""),
(88558,"Young man who just joined military service with heavy training, lightheaded with high urea/creatinine ratio. Diagnosis?",["Pre-renal azotemia","Acute tubular necrosis","Glomerulonephritis","Post-renal obstruction"],"A","Dehydration → pre-renal","",""),
(88559,"9-year-old boy with bedwetting 3 times/week. Management?",["Reassure","Behavioral change","MRI","Refer to urology"],"B","Enuresis ≥ 7 yrs → behavioral first (alarm)","Behavioral therapy (fluid restriction, voiding, alarm) first; desmopressin if fails.","التعليقات: Behavioral change"),
(88560,"12-year-old girl with abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting and diarrhea sometimes streaked with blood. Management?",["Reassure","PPI","Admit for further work-up","Refer to surgery"],"C","Bloody diarrhea → work-up (IBD)","",""),
(88561,"Adult after MVA with blood at the urethral meatus and scrotal ecchymosis. Next step?",["Pyelography","Retrograde urethrogram","Foley catheter"],"B","Blood at meatus → RUG before catheter","Never insert Foley before excluding urethral injury.","التعليقات: retrograde urethrogram"),
(88562,"Child on high-dose steroid treatment came for vaccination. What to do?",["Give DTaP and defer live vaccines","Don't give any vaccine","Give MMR and varicella","Give OPV"],"A","Immunosuppressed → no live vaccines","Inactivated vaccines (DTaP) are fine; defer live (MMR, varicella, OPV) until ≥1 month after stopping high-dose steroids.","التعليقات: A"),
]
